<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Harry S. Truman</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1884">1884</a>–<a href="/years/1972">1972</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Truman</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Harry S. Truman was the 33rd president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, serving from 1945 to 1953. Born in rural Missouri, he fought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> as an artillery captain in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a>, was elected to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-senate">U.S. Senate</a> in 1934 and, as vice president, succeeded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franklin-d-roosevelt">Franklin D. Roosevelt</a> on his death in April 1945. He authorized the first and only use of nuclear weapons in war, against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiroshima">Hiroshima</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasaki">Nagasaki</a>, set up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marshall-plan">Marshall Plan</a>, established the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/truman-doctrine">Truman Doctrine</a> and supported the founding of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> to contain Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a>. He presided over the onset of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin-airlift">Berlin Airlift</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korean-war">Korean War</a>, and issued Executive Order 9981, which desegregated the armed forces.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/harry-s-truman.jpg" alt="Harry S. Truman" width="330" height="445" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: National Archives and Records Administration. Office of Presidential Libraries. Harry S. Truman Library. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TRUMAN_58-766-06_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1948 President <b>Harry S. Truman</b> ordered the desegregation of the armed forces, and the Cold War made segregation an embarrassment for a country seeking allies in Asia and Africa.</blockquote><a href="/movement/civil-rights-movement">Civil rights movement · Background</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Harry Truman</b> became president and presided over the end of the war in Europe and the Pacific.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-12">12 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In his final State of the Union message, <b>Harry Truman</b> disclosed that the US had developed the hydrogen bomb, tested in November 1952.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-7">7 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Johnson signed the Social Security Amendments in Independence, Missouri, with <b>Harry Truman</b> beside him.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-30">30 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Asking Congress for aid to Greece and Turkey, <b>Harry Truman</b> pledged to support 'free peoples who are resisting attempted subjugation'.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-12">12 March in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Harry S Truman Birthplace State Historic Site, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in Research Medical Center, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.5,-94.27&amp;year=1941" aria-label="Harry S Truman Birthplace State Historic Site on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2194 814 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Harry S. Truman"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2644 1084h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2644 1084h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.5,-94.27&amp;year=1941">What was here in 1941? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The seeds of totalitarian regimes are nurtured by misery and want. They spread and grow in the evil soil of poverty and strife. They reach their full growth when the hope of a people for a better life has died.&#8221;</p><footer>Address to Congress (the Truman Doctrine), 12 March 1947 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Truman_Doctrine" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I believe that it must be the policy of the United States to support free peoples who are resisting attempted subjugation by armed minorities or by outside pressures.&#8221;</p><footer>Address to Congress (the Truman Doctrine), 12 March 1947 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Truman_Doctrine" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Communism is based on the belief that man is so weak and inadequate that he is unable to govern himself, and therefore requires the rule of strong masters.&#8221;</p><footer>Inaugural Address (1949) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/925/pg925-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
